I always wanna say mythical dot store.

00:20:27.550 --> 00:20:28.527
Dots com.
It redirects,

00:20:28.527 --> 00:20:30.850
but Mythical.com.

00:20:30.850 --> 00:20:33.610
I'd just really like to
process what's happened

00:20:33.610 --> 00:20:35.480
over the last few weeks.

00:20:35.480 --> 00:20:37.670
How, what have,

00:20:37.670 --> 00:20:39.430
I'm curious what your experience has been

00:20:39.430 --> 00:20:42.420
and I just figure we could
share our experiences,

00:20:42.420 --> 00:20:46.190
because it's just been so surreal.

00:20:46.190 --> 00:20:48.113
There's so many times when I'm like,

00:20:51.100 --> 00:20:53.617
it's like a movie, you know?

00:20:53.617 --> 00:20:55.643
This doesn't really happen.

00:20:58.000 --> 00:20:59.360
I've noticed that that thought

00:20:59.360 --> 00:21:01.020
has started to happen less and less,

00:21:01.020 --> 00:21:04.360
but for the first week and a half or so,

00:21:04.360 --> 00:21:05.900
every night when I'd lay down in the bed

00:21:05.900 --> 00:21:08.300
it was just like, I'd take a breath,

00:21:08.300 --> 00:21:10.420
that would be the main
thing that I would feel

00:21:10.420 --> 00:21:13.030
is like, "Is this real?"

00:21:13.030 --> 00:21:16.510
You know, it was just we were
gettin' pummeled with news,

00:21:16.510 --> 00:21:19.460
every few minutes was
something was changing,

00:21:19.460 --> 00:21:22.620
every one in every sector of society

00:21:22.620 --> 00:21:26.140
was just scrambling to
figure out what to do

00:21:26.140 --> 00:21:30.833
and having the entire
spectrum of reactions.

00:21:32.980 --> 00:21:37.423
I think the one moment
that will stick with me is,

00:21:38.600 --> 00:21:40.980
let's see, it was couple weeks ago

00:21:40.980 --> 00:21:44.760
before things got really stringent here.

00:21:44.760 --> 00:21:47.260
Again, we were trying to
be as safe as possible

00:21:47.260 --> 00:21:51.880
and we were preparing
to send employees home.

00:21:51.880 --> 00:21:54.060
This is Wednesday, a couple of weeks ago.

00:21:54.060 --> 00:21:56.960
And then just getting
'em prepared, like hey,

00:21:56.960 --> 00:21:59.050
Friday, get your stuff, don't come in.

00:21:59.050 --> 00:22:03.040
And then I think the
announcement went out officially

00:22:03.040 --> 00:22:06.017
that Sunday, so I'm not saying,

00:22:06.017 --> 00:22:07.320
"Hey, we were ahead of the curve,"

00:22:07.320 --> 00:22:09.300
but we were sending people home,

00:22:09.300 --> 00:22:12.550
we were starting to process,
but I had not watched the news.

00:22:12.550 --> 00:22:16.560
I had read the news and I
had read the bites of news,

00:22:16.560 --> 00:22:18.610
but we were in a very busy time.

00:22:18.610 --> 00:22:21.263
We were actually recording a lot of GMM,

00:22:22.310 --> 00:22:24.700
and thank goodness we recorded as much

00:22:24.700 --> 00:22:28.100
as we were able to record, to then have

00:22:29.720 --> 00:22:34.549
fully-produced set-based episodes
that we could stretch out.

00:22:34.549 --> 00:22:39.010
But I had not consumed
live television news,

00:22:39.010 --> 00:22:41.630
'cause it's just not a part
of my life anymore, you know?

00:22:41.630 --> 00:22:43.453
I just don't like the bickering,

00:22:45.330 --> 00:22:49.520
it's nothing but stress
and it just ticks me off.

00:22:49.520 --> 00:22:51.220
But I was like hey, we gotta do this,

00:22:51.220 --> 00:22:54.270
so I turned it on, I think
this was like Wednesday

00:22:54.270 --> 00:22:55.910
or Thursday night--
Hold on one second.

00:22:55.910 --> 00:22:57.210
Is the laundry room door closed?

00:22:57.210 --> 00:22:58.450
Yeah, I just closed it!

00:22:58.450 --> 00:23:00.253
Oh, it didn't get any quieter.

00:23:01.460 --> 00:23:02.963
It's okay, go ahead Link.

00:23:04.450 --> 00:23:05.760
It's fine, for what it's worth,

00:23:05.760 --> 00:23:07.910
I can't hear your laundry.
You can't hear it because

00:23:07.910 --> 00:23:11.330
you're listening to a video
chat which is not gonna get it

00:23:11.330 --> 00:23:12.277
but I guarantee you--
That mic's probably gonna--

00:23:12.277 --> 00:23:13.450
And this, we shouldn't have started

00:23:13.450 --> 00:23:16.150
running the washing
machine, but it's fine.

00:23:16.150 --> 00:23:18.190
And by we, you mean...

00:23:19.320 --> 00:23:22.713
I mean my beautiful wife.
Oh, gosh.

00:23:22.713 --> 00:23:23.960
What did you say?

00:23:23.960 --> 00:23:26.343
Go, go finish gettin' ready!

00:23:27.520 --> 00:23:28.455
What did you just say

00:23:28.455 --> 00:23:31.040
about your beautiful wife?
Nothing.

00:23:31.040 --> 00:23:32.010
Okay.

00:23:32.010 --> 00:23:33.693
Yeah, you don't watch news.

00:23:33.693 --> 00:23:36.423
I mean, I watch the news--
I knew that about you.

00:23:38.340 --> 00:23:39.860
I've known that about you for many years

00:23:39.860 --> 00:23:42.130
when we would get to work and I'd be like,

00:23:42.130 --> 00:23:45.100
so did you hear about so-and-so?

00:23:45.100 --> 00:23:45.933
But that makes it--
You know what?

00:23:45.933 --> 00:23:47.540
I changed that!
That recently did change

00:23:47.540 --> 00:23:49.530
because you got like an app--
No, not recently!

00:23:49.530 --> 00:23:54.310
Over the past year, every
day I will read the headlines

00:23:54.310 --> 00:23:56.774
on my phone, I will read the headlines!

00:23:56.774 --> 00:23:58.310
But I'm not gonna turn on--
Hold on, hold on.

00:23:58.310 --> 00:24:01.020
When you've been friends with
somebody for over 35 years,

00:24:01.020 --> 00:24:03.200
recently is in the past
year, just so you--

00:24:03.200 --> 00:24:05.630
Okay, well, that's not
what recently sounds like

00:24:05.630 --> 00:24:08.390
to everybody else.
Just so you know.

00:24:08.390 --> 00:24:13.390
So I turned on the news and it was,

00:24:13.620 --> 00:24:15.760
it's a different experience

00:24:15.760 --> 00:24:18.913
watching television news
than reading about it.

00:24:20.800 --> 00:24:22.530
That's when it really hit me.

00:24:22.530 --> 00:24:25.130
I mean, the newscaster
looked straight down the lens

00:24:25.130 --> 00:24:29.757
and said, "We need to
come to grips with this.

00:24:29.757 --> 00:24:31.797
"You need to come to grips with this.

00:24:31.797 --> 00:24:36.797
"This is the new normal
for the foreseeable future.

00:24:38.554 --> 00:24:41.157
"We're used to getting what we want,

00:24:41.157 --> 00:24:45.437
"when we want it, and that's not

00:24:45.437 --> 00:24:47.520
"going to be the case."

00:24:47.520 --> 00:24:49.482
Jade, chill out.

00:24:49.482 --> 00:24:50.380
I think she's gettin' worked up

00:24:50.380 --> 00:24:51.930
'cause she thinks I'm getting worked up

00:24:51.930 --> 00:24:54.560
because I'm channeling
my inner newscaster.

00:24:54.560 --> 00:24:55.793
What are you barkin' at?

00:24:57.290 --> 00:24:58.870
Do you hear Rhett's laundry?

00:24:58.870 --> 00:24:59.703
It's okay.

00:25:03.816 --> 00:25:06.100
And I gathered the kids around

00:25:06.100 --> 00:25:07.250
'cause Christy and I were watching it,

00:25:07.250 --> 00:25:10.983
and I said, "You know what, kids,

00:25:12.237 --> 00:25:14.487
"I just want you to know
this is a huge deal.

00:25:15.772 --> 00:25:17.800
"We're gonna be as safe as possible."

00:25:17.800 --> 00:25:21.027
I gave them reassurance,
but then I also said,

00:25:21.027 --> 00:25:23.377
"This is something that
you're gonna remember

00:25:23.377 --> 00:25:25.430
"for the rest of your life."

00:25:25.430 --> 00:25:27.330
Hey, am I gonna have to send you away?

00:25:28.440 --> 00:25:30.870
And you know what I mean by that.

00:25:30.870 --> 00:25:32.490
I just mean go in another room

00:25:32.490 --> 00:25:34.623
and sit on another comfy couch.

00:25:38.500 --> 00:25:41.250
The last time I remember
hanging on the words

00:25:41.250 --> 00:25:43.630
of a newscaster was 9/11,

00:25:43.630 --> 00:25:46.560
and I think it was the
governor of New York,

00:25:46.560 --> 00:25:49.510
it may have been thrown
around by a bunch of people

00:25:49.510 --> 00:25:54.510
that this is this generation's 9/11

00:25:55.030 --> 00:25:58.233
in the sense that my experience,

00:25:59.570 --> 00:26:03.230
all of the questions, all
of the fear that came up,

00:26:03.230 --> 00:26:04.313
all of the,

00:26:05.720 --> 00:26:10.140
just not knowing how to process it

00:26:10.140 --> 00:26:12.860
and looking for someone

00:26:12.860 --> 00:26:16.230
or the people who can
help make sense of it

00:26:16.230 --> 00:26:18.463
to give reassurance that there's a plan,

00:26:19.715 --> 00:26:21.950
that we're gonna be okay.

00:26:21.950 --> 00:26:25.110
And I distinctly remember
that morning of 9/11,

00:26:25.110 --> 00:26:28.350
just hanging on every
word of the newscasters,

00:26:28.350 --> 00:26:31.890
and everyone was trying to figure it out

00:26:31.890 --> 00:26:34.530
on live television, you know?
Mm-hmm.

00:26:34.530 --> 00:26:37.470
And again, this was
more drawn-out than that

00:26:37.470 --> 00:26:40.460
and it's different in a bunch of ways,

00:26:40.460 --> 00:26:45.460
but there was a kindred experience

00:26:46.000 --> 00:26:48.863
that then I tried to convey to the kids.

00:26:49.988 --> 00:26:51.947
I just ended up saying,

00:26:51.947 --> 00:26:55.097
"You're gonna remember this
the rest of your lives.

00:26:55.097 --> 00:26:58.187
"Having to hunker down in our homes,"

00:27:01.601 --> 00:27:03.951
I didn't make it that
bleak and doom-and-gloom.

00:27:05.990 --> 00:27:09.260
As scared as I was, I didn't
wanna dump all of that on them

00:27:09.260 --> 00:27:10.160
so I didn't do it.

00:27:12.340 --> 00:27:14.533
Well, the interesting thing, you know,

00:27:16.230 --> 00:27:17.773
I would say two things.

00:27:19.380 --> 00:27:22.650
I come from a slightly
different place with this.

00:27:22.650 --> 00:27:23.483
One,

00:27:26.180 --> 00:27:31.180
my talk about the apocalypse--

00:27:31.770 --> 00:27:32.603
Uh-huh.

00:27:32.603 --> 00:27:35.273
I often talk about it in,

00:27:36.890 --> 00:27:40.060
you know, I'm joking,
I'm going into character

00:27:40.060 --> 00:27:43.150
and I'm just kinda BS-ing.

00:27:43.150 --> 00:27:47.530
But that comes from a
place of actual concern

00:27:47.530 --> 00:27:49.773
that, you know,

00:27:51.580 --> 00:27:53.690
all our systems are so fragile.

00:27:53.690 --> 00:27:56.520
We're seeing that right
now, we're seeing that

00:28:00.080 --> 00:28:02.850
we were a lot more
vulnerable in a lot more ways

00:28:02.850 --> 00:28:06.590
than anybody, well, I'm
gonna say than anybody knew,

00:28:06.590 --> 00:28:08.590
but actually there's a lot of people

00:28:08.590 --> 00:28:11.010
who've been saying for years, you know?

00:28:11.010 --> 00:28:15.590
Again, I think people
who aren't in America

00:28:15.590 --> 00:28:17.810
might be a little bit perplexed

00:28:17.810 --> 00:28:21.760
as to what they hear
coming out of the U.S.

00:28:21.760 --> 00:28:23.750
and they see on Twitter
and they see on the news,

00:28:23.750 --> 00:28:25.240
it's like--
Mm-hmm, mm-hmm.

00:28:25.240 --> 00:28:29.830
Why is there this very bifurcated reaction

00:28:29.830 --> 00:28:32.480
to what's happening, and
why does it seem like

00:28:32.480 --> 00:28:35.290
people of a particular
political persuasion

00:28:35.290 --> 00:28:38.890
seem to think a certain way about a virus?

00:28:38.890 --> 00:28:41.440
I think that's probably rather perplexing

00:28:41.440 --> 00:28:43.493
to people from other places in the world.

00:28:46.013 --> 00:28:48.660
And there's a lotta reasons
that go behind that.

00:28:48.660 --> 00:28:50.730
But I think that, interestingly,

00:28:50.730 --> 00:28:52.937
we have seen a lot of people saying,

00:28:52.937 --> 00:28:54.870
"Hey, guys, listen, this
is going to happen."

00:28:54.870 --> 00:28:56.027
You see a lot of people saying like,

00:28:56.027 --> 00:28:57.880
"Nobody could have been
prepared for this."

00:28:57.880 --> 00:29:00.710
Well actually, there's
some excellent books

00:29:00.710 --> 00:29:02.960
that have been written about
this that basically said

00:29:02.960 --> 00:29:06.970
that this was completely
and totally inevitable,

00:29:06.970 --> 00:29:09.430
unavoidable, predictable.

00:29:09.430 --> 00:29:12.870
They've been telling us for years that

00:29:12.870 --> 00:29:15.170
given how global our society is now

00:29:15.170 --> 00:29:17.250
and how quickly people travel

00:29:17.250 --> 00:29:20.380
and how easy it is for a virus to jump

00:29:20.380 --> 00:29:22.240
from an animal to a person

00:29:22.240 --> 00:29:25.390
and there being no immunity, anyway,

00:29:25.390 --> 00:29:27.440
we kinda knew that this was gonna happen.

00:29:27.440 --> 00:29:31.330
So I think I kinda had
in the many different

00:29:31.330 --> 00:29:34.167
end-time scenarios that when you watch

00:29:34.167 --> 00:29:35.920
"Doomsday Preppers" and that kinda thing.

00:29:35.920 --> 00:29:37.050
And again, I don't think that,

00:29:37.050 --> 00:29:40.060
we're not in an apocalyptic
situation by any means.

00:29:40.060 --> 00:29:42.470
This is not the end of culture or humanity

00:29:42.470 --> 00:29:44.860
or anything, this just a difficult thing

00:29:44.860 --> 00:29:46.820
that is affecting everybody
and we have to kind of

00:29:46.820 --> 00:29:49.480
come together and overcome
and we will overcome.

00:29:49.480 --> 00:29:51.808
It's just a matter of
how long will it take?

00:29:51.808 --> 00:29:54.110
But you're the type of person

00:29:54.110 --> 00:29:57.990
that was familiar with the world

00:29:57.990 --> 00:30:02.020
of the world going into crisis because

00:30:03.420 --> 00:30:07.050
you have an interest in it
because there's somethin',

00:30:07.050 --> 00:30:08.710
because of your personality
type there's something

00:30:08.710 --> 00:30:12.160
within you that made you read about it,

00:30:12.160 --> 00:30:14.040
but then there's a
second step that's like,

00:30:14.040 --> 00:30:15.310
when you would read about these things

00:30:15.310 --> 00:30:16.710
you would process them in a way

00:30:16.710 --> 00:30:18.653
that led to personal application.

00:30:20.130 --> 00:30:21.962
I think it was the GMM
episode where you went through

00:30:21.962 --> 00:30:25.520
all the apocalypse bunker
stuff that you had bought,

00:30:25.520 --> 00:30:26.470
and you took me through it,

00:30:26.470 --> 00:30:30.550
and then you also bought
stuff for me, right?

00:30:30.550 --> 00:30:32.700
Which hopefully you still have.

00:30:32.700 --> 00:30:35.410
I still have, I haven't
had to dig into it yet

00:30:35.410 --> 00:30:36.910
but I feel better knowing I have it,

00:30:36.910 --> 00:30:38.740
so thank you once again.

00:30:38.740 --> 00:30:40.430
But yeah, I think we
represent two different

00:30:40.430 --> 00:30:42.333
personality types that, like,

00:30:44.959 --> 00:30:47.053
and I think mines a little bit different,

00:30:49.250 --> 00:30:50.183
but go ahead.

00:30:51.100 --> 00:30:53.830
So yeah, this was something
that wasn't a surprise

00:30:53.830 --> 00:30:56.000
to me, right?
Mm-hmm.

00:30:56.000 --> 00:30:57.690
But the second thing was

00:31:01.160 --> 00:31:03.717
we have the news on all the time.

00:31:03.717 --> 00:31:05.220
It's kind of like,

00:31:06.610 --> 00:31:11.030
we've got the news on
in the house at night

00:31:11.030 --> 00:31:14.850
a couple, three times a
week, I think our kids,

00:31:14.850 --> 00:31:18.010
our kids get very tired
of hearing me and Jessie

00:31:18.010 --> 00:31:20.790
talk about the news.

00:31:20.790 --> 00:31:22.900
Whereas with Lando, when I sat him down

00:31:22.900 --> 00:31:24.940
and we were watching the news that night

00:31:24.940 --> 00:31:26.640
it was the first time we
had ever watched the news

00:31:26.640 --> 00:31:30.590
as a family, and the newscaster
was interviewing somebody

00:31:30.590 --> 00:31:33.570
and they were getting
into it, like they do.

00:31:33.570 --> 00:31:34.830
They were trying to cut each other off

00:31:34.830 --> 00:31:37.630
and one guy wanted to
say what he wanted to say

00:31:37.630 --> 00:31:39.930
instead of answer the question.

00:31:39.930 --> 00:31:41.773
And Lando turned to me and said,

00:31:42.617 --> 00:31:46.113
"Are they angry with each other?

00:31:46.113 --> 00:31:48.910
"Are they fighting?" and I was like, yeah,

00:31:48.910 --> 00:31:52.663
that's what happens on the
news, which was news to him.

00:31:53.900 --> 00:31:56.543
Again, that's not my vibe, man.

00:31:59.260 --> 00:32:00.900
I gotta watch the Australian news.

00:32:00.900 --> 00:32:02.770
I think that's one of
the interesting elements

00:32:02.770 --> 00:32:03.900
of this thing is that

00:32:06.855 --> 00:32:10.093
because the whole world of news,

00:32:11.730 --> 00:32:16.060
online, print and cable news,

00:32:16.060 --> 00:32:18.810
everything is driven by ad revenue, right?

00:32:18.810 --> 00:32:21.050
So for years we've gotten into this place

00:32:21.050 --> 00:32:23.861
where it seems like every time
you come back from a break

00:32:23.861 --> 00:32:28.861
on a cable news program,
breaking news, right?

00:32:29.070 --> 00:32:32.400
And I think when things
actually become breaking news,

00:32:32.400 --> 00:32:34.510
like they have right now,

00:32:34.510 --> 00:32:37.230
when people actually need to know,

00:32:37.230 --> 00:32:39.610
like it's a potential
life or death situation

00:32:39.610 --> 00:32:41.660
whether or not they get this information,

00:32:42.500 --> 00:32:46.120
I think people have maybe
become a little bit numb to

00:32:46.120 --> 00:32:48.020
like, "Oh yeah, you guys are
panicking about something."

00:32:48.020 --> 00:32:49.410
I think that's one of the
things that's happening

00:32:49.410 --> 00:32:51.587
in our country, a lot
of people who are like,

00:32:51.587 --> 00:32:53.607
"They're always talking
about the latest crisis.

00:32:53.607 --> 00:32:56.297
"They're always talking about
how things are falling apart,

00:32:56.297 --> 00:32:58.480
"but does it ever really happen?"

00:32:58.480 --> 00:33:01.210
So I think that people
are skeptical of that.

00:33:01.210 --> 00:33:03.487
You've even got the Nat Geo shows, like,

00:33:03.487 --> 00:33:05.927
"The seven ways that the world will end

00:33:05.927 --> 00:33:10.310
"and let's spend 60 minutes
on each one," you know?

00:33:10.310 --> 00:33:12.460
And that's just not the type
of stuff I like to watch.

00:33:12.460 --> 00:33:14.350
You like to watch that type of stuff.

00:33:14.350 --> 00:33:15.730
Because I don't want,

00:33:15.730 --> 00:33:19.070
I would rather think, and
on a subconscious level

00:33:19.070 --> 00:33:20.963
it's been easy for me to think,

00:33:22.680 --> 00:33:25.810
scientists or experts
in whatever field it is,

00:33:25.810 --> 00:33:28.501
they have to be alarmists
in order to be heard.

00:33:28.501 --> 00:33:30.880
You've gotta be your own hype man

00:33:30.880 --> 00:33:33.740
if you want anybody to listen to you.

00:33:33.740 --> 00:33:38.700
And it was a convenient
position subconsciously

00:33:38.700 --> 00:33:43.120
for me to settle into, but I will say

00:33:43.120 --> 00:33:47.150
turning on the news and
experiencing the breaking news

00:33:47.150 --> 00:33:49.630
for the first time, because
I was out of that world,

00:33:49.630 --> 00:33:50.773
I woke up.

00:33:51.901 --> 00:33:53.730
I was texting you that night.

00:33:53.730 --> 00:33:57.450
I was like, what does
this mean for our friends,

00:33:57.450 --> 00:33:59.400
our families, for our company

00:33:59.400 --> 00:34:01.740
in terms of action we need to take.

00:34:01.740 --> 00:34:03.540
You were already talking about that,

00:34:04.420 --> 00:34:06.663
so I felt like I kinda got on board.

00:34:08.450 --> 00:34:10.350
And then there's people who are still,

00:34:11.249 --> 00:34:14.140
it's had to sink in for
everyone in their own time

00:34:14.140 --> 00:34:16.470
and that's the difficulty,
because in the meantime

00:34:16.470 --> 00:34:17.893
this virus is movin' around.

00:34:20.910 --> 00:34:24.160
But I think I did get on board
and I think you were like,

00:34:24.160 --> 00:34:26.620
was there any part of you that was like,

00:34:26.620 --> 00:34:29.610
I'll call it a morbid excitement.

00:34:29.610 --> 00:34:30.763
Like yes, this is,

00:34:32.330 --> 00:34:33.900
I don't want to frame you here,

00:34:33.900 --> 00:34:37.488
maybe that's a nasty question.

00:34:37.488 --> 00:34:39.063
Well,

00:34:40.470 --> 00:34:41.893
I think that,

00:34:46.577 --> 00:34:49.710
actually, I think it's
helpful sometimes because,

00:34:49.710 --> 00:34:52.220
I'll talk in a second about
what I'm actually fearful of

00:34:52.220 --> 00:34:54.280
and what I'm anxious about,

00:34:54.280 --> 00:34:56.323
how I've been dealing
with that personally.

00:34:58.060 --> 00:35:00.410
But to me, I find it helpful

00:35:00.410 --> 00:35:01.800
when I'm going through
something that would

00:35:01.800 --> 00:35:06.623
otherwise be fear-inducing
to kind of think about,

00:35:08.520 --> 00:35:13.180
to think about being
in the midst of a story

00:35:13.180 --> 00:35:15.153
that has a happy ending, right?

00:35:16.220 --> 00:35:18.690
To think about what role you're playing in

00:35:20.520 --> 00:35:22.310
this larger adventure of humanity

00:35:22.310 --> 00:35:24.580
kind of coming together
and overcoming this thing.

00:35:24.580 --> 00:35:25.413
So there is,

00:35:26.777 --> 00:35:27.660
and I don't wanna talk about it now

00:35:27.660 --> 00:35:29.170
'cause I think there's
a whole other podcast

00:35:29.170 --> 00:35:33.690
in talking about why this
could ultimately be good

00:35:33.690 --> 00:35:36.410
for humanity, experiencing
a crisis like this.

00:35:36.410 --> 00:35:37.600
We could talk about all the things

00:35:37.600 --> 00:35:39.343
that might change for the better.

00:35:40.980 --> 00:35:43.590
I know you've been watching
"Lord of the Rings"

00:35:43.590 --> 00:35:45.980
as well, right?
That's right, yeah.

00:35:45.980 --> 00:35:49.420
So as a family, everybody in the family's

00:35:49.420 --> 00:35:52.490
already seen the movies,
but it's been a while

00:35:52.490 --> 00:35:55.860
and so far we've just watched
"Fellowship of the Ring"

00:35:55.860 --> 00:35:56.917
and "The Two Towers," we haven't watched

00:35:56.917 --> 00:35:58.360
"Return of the King" yet.

00:35:58.360 --> 00:36:02.240
But you had to be thinking
the same thing I was thinking

00:36:02.240 --> 00:36:04.850
multiple times during...

00:36:08.750 --> 00:36:10.610
This could be my rec,
but I have another rec,

00:36:10.610 --> 00:36:14.010
but I'll make a mid-podcast rec

00:36:14.010 --> 00:36:16.160
of watching "The Lord
of the Rings" because

00:36:17.840 --> 00:36:19.000
first of all, like you said,

00:36:19.000 --> 00:36:22.177
you remember how much better
it is than "The Hobbit," right?

00:36:22.177 --> 00:36:24.820
"The Hobbit" was a great book,

00:36:24.820 --> 00:36:28.457
but the translation to three
movies, it just didn't,

00:36:28.457 --> 00:36:30.373
"The Lord of the Rings" is,

00:36:32.274 --> 00:36:34.677
it's my favorite trilogy, okay?

00:36:34.677 --> 00:36:36.727
That I'm on, this is my favorite trilogy.

00:36:37.680 --> 00:36:39.140
When I was watching it, I was like,

00:36:39.140 --> 00:36:41.788
this is better than "Star Wars."

00:36:41.788 --> 00:36:43.610
And Lily still wanted to fight me--

00:36:43.610 --> 00:36:45.478
You know I've always thought that.

00:36:45.478 --> 00:36:46.311
I know you've always thought that

00:36:46.311 --> 00:36:49.367
but I had forgotten, it was
nice to get back into it.

00:36:49.367 --> 00:36:50.616
But yeah, it was--

00:36:50.616 --> 00:36:53.467
I don't weep while watching "Star Wars."

00:36:54.430 --> 00:36:59.430
There's so many moments of
just pure joy and sadness.

00:37:01.990 --> 00:37:02.940
Anyway.
Yeah.

00:37:02.940 --> 00:37:05.870
But there's these things that
Frodo, and Sam especially,

00:37:05.870 --> 00:37:08.560
and Gandalf, they kinda say to each other

00:37:08.560 --> 00:37:12.970
that are these epic inspirational phrases,

00:37:14.360 --> 00:37:15.990
there's these conversations that they have

00:37:15.990 --> 00:37:17.790
where they start realizing, in fact,

00:37:19.110 --> 00:37:20.960
is it at the end of "The
Fellowship of the Ring"

00:37:20.960 --> 00:37:24.380
or is it "The Two Towers" where,

00:37:24.380 --> 00:37:27.330
I think it's in "Two Towers"
where Sam starts talking about

00:37:28.350 --> 00:37:30.223
are they gonna tell stories about us?

00:37:31.970 --> 00:37:33.340
You know?

00:37:33.340 --> 00:37:35.730
I think that might be the
end of the first movie.

00:37:35.730 --> 00:37:38.570
Yeah, but it's like, are
we gonna be the heroes

00:37:38.570 --> 00:37:41.010
in the story, and I'm not--
Right.

00:37:41.010 --> 00:37:43.100
I like to think about
situations like this,

00:37:43.100 --> 00:37:45.130
not like how am I gonna be a hero,

00:37:45.130 --> 00:37:49.140
but how are we as a species
going to overcome this

00:37:49.140 --> 00:37:51.260
and what's the story
that we're gonna tell?

00:37:51.260 --> 00:37:52.810
And I hope the story that we tell

00:37:52.810 --> 00:37:56.350
isn't that we started
not trusting one another

00:37:56.350 --> 00:37:58.510
and we just were completely polarized.

00:37:58.510 --> 00:38:01.283
This is an opportunity to come together.

00:38:02.210 --> 00:38:05.400
Again, I so think it
requires people to actually

00:38:06.500 --> 00:38:08.800
sorta digest what the real threat is,

00:38:08.800 --> 00:38:11.930
and I think that even by the
time this podcast comes out,

00:38:11.930 --> 00:38:13.227
that many more people will be like,

00:38:13.227 --> 00:38:15.817
"Oh, okay, this is serious."

00:38:18.470 --> 00:38:19.393
So I do think there's a part of me--

00:38:19.393 --> 00:38:22.210
I find myself gleaning lots of hope

00:38:22.210 --> 00:38:24.115
from watching the movies.
Yeah.

00:38:24.115 --> 00:38:28.210
It's like the hope that
drove Sam and Frodo

00:38:28.210 --> 00:38:30.650
on their journey and
that drove everyone else

00:38:30.650 --> 00:38:32.310
to do whatever they could,

00:38:32.310 --> 00:38:37.090
even when they couldn't
help Frodo directly anymore

00:38:37.090 --> 00:38:39.150
they could help out their friends.

00:38:39.150 --> 00:38:43.210
They could help out those
that they were with.

00:38:43.210 --> 00:38:44.043
Yeah.

00:38:45.240 --> 00:38:47.840
Or say, okay, we can't go after them

00:38:47.840 --> 00:38:50.403
but we can go after Merry
and Pippin, you know?

00:38:52.315 --> 00:38:57.315
And it hit me at such a point
where I really needed it.

00:38:58.210 --> 00:39:00.350
I don't think I've ever
gleaned so much hope

00:39:00.350 --> 00:39:04.020
from watching a movie
that applied directly to,

00:39:04.020 --> 00:39:08.890
it just kind of fueled my ability

00:39:10.006 --> 00:39:10.940
to get up the next day

00:39:10.940 --> 00:39:14.040
and be positive.
I think that's good for us.

00:39:14.040 --> 00:39:14.873
Yeah.

00:39:14.873 --> 00:39:16.460
That's the kind of stuff that,

00:39:17.360 --> 00:39:20.410
those are the kind of
challenges that we need to face,

00:39:20.410 --> 00:39:22.360
that we need to come together and face.

00:39:23.730 --> 00:39:25.140
Specifically, the way that I've kind of

00:39:25.140 --> 00:39:29.423
been responding to it
in terms of how this,

00:39:30.830 --> 00:39:34.030
the virus kind of taps
into my hypochondria--

00:39:34.030 --> 00:39:35.633
Okay.
Has been interesting.

00:39:36.550 --> 00:39:39.370
Even though I know from
a statistical standpoint

00:39:40.760 --> 00:39:42.640
the majority of people who get it

00:39:42.640 --> 00:39:44.090
won't even know they have it,

00:39:46.010 --> 00:39:48.290
and then on top of that

00:39:48.290 --> 00:39:50.300
a larger majority of people who get it

00:39:50.300 --> 00:39:53.610
will not require hospitalization.

00:39:53.610 --> 00:39:55.220
And then within our age group,

00:39:55.220 --> 00:39:57.070
with no underlying health conditions,

00:39:58.240 --> 00:40:01.120
again, statistically, we're
probably gonna be okay

00:40:01.120 --> 00:40:03.223
even if we contract this thing, right?

00:40:05.450 --> 00:40:07.350
But you hear those stories, right?

00:40:07.350 --> 00:40:09.291
I think a lot of the
stories that we're hearing,

00:40:09.291 --> 00:40:12.770
and I think that these
are getting more attention

00:40:12.770 --> 00:40:13.887
because they're surprising, it's like,

00:40:13.887 --> 00:40:17.857
"Oh, 31-year-old Olympic
gold medalist swimmer

00:40:17.857 --> 00:40:20.257
"just dealt with this for two weeks

00:40:20.257 --> 00:40:23.140
"and is still feeling the effects,"

00:40:23.140 --> 00:40:28.140
or, "34-year-old dude in
L.A. dies from COVID-19."

00:40:31.740 --> 00:40:33.900
So there's a part of me that, I mean,

00:40:33.900 --> 00:40:37.270
of course I'm thinking about my parents

00:40:37.270 --> 00:40:40.520
who are in the higher-risk group--

00:40:40.520 --> 00:40:42.960
Yeah.
Your parents, you know,

00:40:42.960 --> 00:40:46.241
we know a lot of people who
are in higher-risk groups--

00:40:46.241 --> 00:40:48.240
And I got my, both of my grandmothers.

00:40:48.240 --> 00:40:49.410
Yeah, you've still got grandparents

00:40:49.410 --> 00:40:51.479
who are alive in their 90s.

00:40:51.479 --> 00:40:52.312
Yeah.

00:40:52.312 --> 00:40:54.683
So thinking about them,

00:40:55.870 --> 00:40:59.237
but then I start thinking,
"Oh, I think I have,

00:40:59.237 --> 00:41:00.707
"do I have a dry cough?"

00:41:01.600 --> 00:41:06.380
You know, I'll start thinking,
"Do my lungs feel funny?"

00:41:06.380 --> 00:41:09.420
Like that's how my hypochondria
kind of manifests itself

00:41:09.420 --> 00:41:12.923
and I start thinking that,
"Have I contracted this?

00:41:14.757 --> 00:41:17.030
"Am I gonna be one of the statistics?"

00:41:17.030 --> 00:41:20.440
So I kinda get on a little
bit of a fear loop--

00:41:20.440 --> 00:41:23.103
Spiral.
Doing that.

00:41:25.441 --> 00:41:28.320
And it hasn't helped
that I've also, I think,

00:41:28.320 --> 00:41:30.770
now I already knew that I was
slightly allergic to dogs.

00:41:30.770 --> 00:41:33.670
Like when I got my allergy
test a couple years ago

00:41:33.670 --> 00:41:36.550
the only thing that came
up was a slight allergy

00:41:36.550 --> 00:41:38.270
to dogs and cats,

00:41:38.270 --> 00:41:40.080
but it was like a two
out of five or something.

00:41:40.080 --> 00:41:43.295
But COVID-19 does not give you

00:41:43.295 --> 00:41:45.400
an allergic reaction to dogs.

00:41:45.400 --> 00:41:50.400
Right, but because I am now
at home with Barbara a lot,

00:41:52.140 --> 00:41:54.070
spending a lot of time with Barbara,

00:41:54.070 --> 00:41:56.330
it's like I've had this
drainage and sore throat

00:41:56.330 --> 00:41:58.330
that's been going on like two weeks now,

00:41:59.750 --> 00:42:02.575
and I'm reasonably certain it's just--

00:42:02.575 --> 00:42:05.530
That it's COVID-19.
That's what I thought,

00:42:05.530 --> 00:42:07.110
even though those are not really symptoms

00:42:07.110 --> 00:42:09.580
of COVID-19 for most people.

00:42:09.580 --> 00:42:11.067
So up until like yesterday when it hit me,

00:42:11.067 --> 00:42:14.017
I was like, "Oh, this might
be because me and Barbara

00:42:14.017 --> 00:42:16.160
"are having so much quality time,"

00:42:16.160 --> 00:42:18.813
which I've really enjoyed all
my quality time with Barbara.

00:42:20.100 --> 00:42:21.230
I don't know what I'm gonna do about that.

00:42:21.230 --> 00:42:23.630
I might have to get a
better allergy medication.

00:42:24.630 --> 00:42:27.323
But anyway--
You could shave her bald.

00:42:29.500 --> 00:42:30.780
I thought you were gonna
say something else,

00:42:30.780 --> 00:42:33.550
and then I was gonna say
that she's a female dog.

00:42:33.550 --> 00:42:34.383
Bald.

00:42:37.809 --> 00:42:40.607
You know where the allergens come from,

00:42:40.607 --> 00:42:43.650
the come from the scrotum of the dog.

00:42:43.650 --> 00:42:45.000
The hair on the scrotum.

00:42:45.000 --> 00:42:48.140
But my wife has the exact opposite issue,

00:42:48.140 --> 00:42:51.420
and she thinks that she's
going to give it to someone,

00:42:51.420 --> 00:42:53.120
right?
Okay.

00:42:53.120 --> 00:42:54.790
So I'm worried about getting it,

00:42:54.790 --> 00:42:57.003
she's worrying about giving it.

00:42:58.221 --> 00:42:59.840
Well that should make
you feel better, right?

00:42:59.840 --> 00:43:02.460
That she's not gonna give it to you.

00:43:02.460 --> 00:43:03.555
Well, I don't think she's worried about--

00:43:03.555 --> 00:43:05.340
Is she wearin' a mask everywhere?

00:43:05.340 --> 00:43:07.470
No, no.
Okay.

00:43:07.470 --> 00:43:11.030
But as you know, I do have masks

00:43:11.030 --> 00:43:12.080
because that was one of the things

00:43:12.080 --> 00:43:14.130
that I had already bought many years ago.

00:43:16.548 --> 00:43:19.197
I don't have many, I have like
a couple of packages of 'em,

00:43:20.700 --> 00:43:25.300
because you don't need 'em
unless you're sick, technically.

00:43:25.300 --> 00:43:27.650
We were gonna have just
a couple for ourselves.

00:43:28.660 --> 00:43:31.702
If you have an N95 mask it
can offer some protection

00:43:31.702 --> 00:43:34.080
if it's sealed correctly,
which is pretty hard

00:43:34.080 --> 00:43:36.000
to do with a beard like this.

00:43:36.000 --> 00:43:37.530
Like if you're gonna
go into a grocery store

00:43:37.530 --> 00:43:39.520
and you want to be extra safe

00:43:39.520 --> 00:43:41.290
it's not gonna hurt to wear a mask.

00:43:41.290 --> 00:43:45.847
But Jessie's like, "Hey,
we need to take these masks

00:43:45.847 --> 00:43:48.380
"to the hospital," so
she's already talking

00:43:48.380 --> 00:43:51.479
to this person on Twitter,

00:43:51.479 --> 00:43:54.830
this is like three, four days ago.

00:43:54.830 --> 00:43:56.070
And again, this is like two weeks out

00:43:56.070 --> 00:43:57.380
from when you're gonna see this episode.

00:43:57.380 --> 00:44:00.150
But in Santa Monica there was an ER doctor

00:44:00.150 --> 00:44:03.317
who was already saying,
"We're running out of masks.

00:44:03.317 --> 00:44:05.277
"I will drive anywhere
in L.A. to pick them up,

00:44:05.277 --> 00:44:06.927
"I don't care how many you have,"

00:44:06.927 --> 00:44:11.210
and so she was talking to
this person on Twitter.

00:44:11.210 --> 00:44:13.960
But at the time she
kinda had a sore throat

00:44:13.960 --> 00:44:15.387
and she was like, "I
just wanna let you know,

00:44:15.387 --> 00:44:16.970
"full disclosure, I have a sore throat,"

00:44:16.970 --> 00:44:18.590
and the woman was like,
okay, wait 14 days.

00:44:18.590 --> 00:44:23.000
Anyway, we're probably gonna
give our masks to a hospital,

00:44:23.000 --> 00:44:24.410
as you should if you have some

00:44:24.410 --> 00:44:26.470
if you're one of the people that,

00:44:26.470 --> 00:44:28.263
like me, had some ahead of time.

00:44:29.130 --> 00:44:31.643
I think for me it's, you know,

00:44:34.024 --> 00:44:36.430
I don't obsess about if I have it or not.

00:44:36.430 --> 00:44:40.313
I feel like for over a week now already

00:44:40.313 --> 00:44:43.390
we've been isolated, we've
been very strict about it,

00:44:43.390 --> 00:44:45.710
so with every day I'm
gaining more confidence

00:44:45.710 --> 00:44:48.080
that we're not carrying it,

00:44:48.080 --> 00:44:50.730
and we're doing everything in our power

00:44:50.730 --> 00:44:53.563
to protect ourselves and
other people at the same time.

00:44:55.420 --> 00:44:59.500
So for me it's more about

00:45:00.520 --> 00:45:05.520
channeling my energies into
a routine, really routine.

00:45:06.820 --> 00:45:08.500
Things that I know like I'm gonna do this

00:45:08.500 --> 00:45:09.333
and I'm gonna do this
and I'm gonna do this

00:45:09.333 --> 00:45:11.050
and I'm gonna fill my day with things,

00:45:11.050 --> 00:45:13.423
I'm gonna occupy myself.

00:45:15.660 --> 00:45:18.560
As we've been developing
what our work plan is,

00:45:18.560 --> 00:45:19.420
that's certainly helped,

00:45:19.420 --> 00:45:22.230
but before we really got on top of that

00:45:22.230 --> 00:45:25.550
there were a few days there
where it was just like

00:45:25.550 --> 00:45:28.270
a lotta cleaning, you know?

00:45:28.270 --> 00:45:31.980
Yesterday I went in, I
always go into my shower

00:45:31.980 --> 00:45:36.050
and as I said in the You're
Not Showering Properly

00:45:36.050 --> 00:45:38.120
GMM episode or whatever it's titled,

00:45:38.120 --> 00:45:39.833
we change the titles so much.

00:45:42.380 --> 00:45:44.150
I will clean the drain of the shower

00:45:44.150 --> 00:45:46.250
because there's so much freakin'
hair that gets in there.

00:45:46.250 --> 00:45:47.520
I don't know how my wife has any

00:45:47.520 --> 00:45:49.133
on her head anymore, honestly.

00:45:50.540 --> 00:45:54.483
My shower won't drain unless
I clean the hair out of it,

00:45:55.510 --> 00:45:56.870
and I'm blaming it all on her

00:45:56.870 --> 00:45:59.050
because she's not currently here.

00:45:59.050 --> 00:46:01.900
Her and Lily just walked out
to take a walk or somethin'.

00:46:04.300 --> 00:46:05.540
But the way my shower works,

00:46:05.540 --> 00:46:09.900
it's like this fancy,
long, heavy strip of tile

00:46:09.900 --> 00:46:13.580
that's like a, it's like a one-inch tall,

00:46:13.580 --> 00:46:15.053
two-inch wide,

00:46:16.310 --> 00:46:20.170
four-inch deep, like slab,

00:46:20.170 --> 00:46:23.130
that you have to pull
out, because it looks,

00:46:23.130 --> 00:46:24.958
it looks fancy, right,
you can't see the drain

00:46:24.958 --> 00:46:26.820
and then the water drains all in it.

00:46:26.820 --> 00:46:31.700
And when you pull out this
big freakin' rectangular slab

00:46:33.989 --> 00:46:36.390
there's one little drain at the end,

00:46:36.390 --> 00:46:40.560
just one silver dollar drain
with like a thing on it.

00:46:40.560 --> 00:46:42.550
But that whole trough is plastic,

00:46:42.550 --> 00:46:45.333
and it fills up with soap gunk.

00:46:46.340 --> 00:46:49.090
And so here I am for the first time ever,

00:46:49.090 --> 00:46:50.770
not just cleaning the
hair out of the drain

00:46:50.770 --> 00:46:52.250
but I'm gettin' down on all fours

00:46:52.250 --> 00:46:54.950
and I'm spraying stuff down,
I'm grabbin' a hand towel

00:46:54.950 --> 00:46:58.883
and I'm cleaning out the
trough of this thing.

00:47:00.530 --> 00:47:01.363
Felt good.

00:47:02.320 --> 00:47:05.373
Didn't feel necessary
but it felt very good.

00:47:06.650 --> 00:47:10.510
Even though I totally destroyed
my hand towel with bleach.

00:47:10.510 --> 00:47:12.713
It's like a pink mosaic now.

00:47:13.760 --> 00:47:14.593
That's nice.

00:47:15.970 --> 00:47:17.500
Not really, it's pretty ugly.

00:47:17.500 --> 00:47:21.490
We've been doing a lot of cleaning,

00:47:21.490 --> 00:47:26.490
and getting to things that over time,

00:47:27.276 --> 00:47:31.540
like the area next to my
bed, like my bedside table

00:47:32.930 --> 00:47:36.240
I had stacked books and
electronic equipment

00:47:36.240 --> 00:47:40.240
and stuff and it just slowly,
my wife calls it my corner.

00:47:40.240 --> 00:47:41.180
She's like, "When are you
gonna clean your corner?"

00:47:41.180 --> 00:47:42.680
You have one at the office too.

00:47:42.680 --> 00:47:46.657
And I'm like, "Well, when I have time."

00:47:47.555 --> 00:47:48.549
'Cause it's not that important,

00:47:48.549 --> 00:47:49.870
it's not important to clean the corner.

00:47:49.870 --> 00:47:52.260
Well, the corner is cleaned.

00:47:52.260 --> 00:47:53.710
The corner is very clean right now,

00:47:53.710 --> 00:47:54.669
you could sit in my corner--

00:47:54.669 --> 00:47:55.870
Did you take a before and after photo?

00:47:55.870 --> 00:47:57.510
No, I don't do that kind of thing.

00:47:57.510 --> 00:48:00.010
I don't need to congratulate
myself with pictures.

00:48:02.780 --> 00:48:03.930
Uh, you know what?

00:48:03.930 --> 00:48:07.130
I'm not gonna take that
because it's your loss

00:48:07.130 --> 00:48:09.360
that you don't want to stop and celebrate

00:48:09.360 --> 00:48:10.760
your own accomplishments.

00:48:10.760 --> 00:48:12.660
I don't wanna become addicted to it.

00:48:14.340 --> 00:48:15.920
Of course that doesn't happen.

00:48:15.920 --> 00:48:18.580
Who's addicted to
cleaning and organization?

00:48:18.580 --> 00:48:19.541
Exactly.

00:48:19.541 --> 00:48:22.730
Who finds themselves trapped
by their own processes?

00:48:22.730 --> 00:48:23.900
Right.
No one.

00:48:23.900 --> 00:48:27.380
The think that I've been thinking,

00:48:27.380 --> 00:48:28.713
so we have been,

00:48:31.230 --> 00:48:33.963
from a both personal and
professional standpoint,

00:48:36.404 --> 00:48:39.516
this has shaken things up,
changed things around, right?

00:48:39.516 --> 00:48:41.100
Completely, really.

00:48:41.100 --> 00:48:44.520
My heart goes out to the many people

00:48:44.520 --> 00:48:49.520
whose businesses just completely
stopped because of this.

00:48:49.820 --> 00:48:52.290
And I understand that
that's one of the big

00:48:52.290 --> 00:48:54.140
national debates happening right now.

00:48:57.420 --> 00:48:59.040
And we can talk a little
bit more about this

00:48:59.040 --> 00:49:01.250
maybe in a subsequent podcast, about like

00:49:02.530 --> 00:49:05.190
weighing the value of the economy

00:49:05.190 --> 00:49:07.490
versus the number of deaths, which to me

00:49:07.490 --> 00:49:10.300
seems like sort of a weird thing to weigh.

00:49:10.300 --> 00:49:14.860
But I understand that
a complete depression,

00:49:14.860 --> 00:49:18.240
economic depression, will
harm people and make people,

00:49:18.240 --> 00:49:21.353
it will lead to, ultimately,
more death in some ways.

00:49:21.353 --> 00:49:24.053
So it is a factor and I'm
sensitive to the people who,

00:49:26.450 --> 00:49:28.360
they don't have any way
to make money right now.

00:49:28.360 --> 00:49:32.340
We're in a position where
we can still bring content

00:49:32.340 --> 00:49:35.090
to people even if it's
slightly altered like this,

00:49:35.090 --> 00:49:38.920
but assuming that the ad
market is still going to be

00:49:38.920 --> 00:49:42.420
somewhat, is gonna to exist on some level.

00:49:42.420 --> 00:49:44.780
We still are in a service business,

00:49:44.780 --> 00:49:46.830
we're offering a service that we think

00:49:46.830 --> 00:49:48.300
is helpful at this time,

00:49:48.300 --> 00:49:49.820
and so we're committed to doing it,

00:49:49.820 --> 00:49:53.160
but thankfully we're in a
position where we can do that

00:49:53.160 --> 00:49:56.200
and keep our employees employed
and that kind of thing.

00:49:56.200 --> 00:49:57.410
And not everybody's in that position,

00:49:57.410 --> 00:49:59.310
so we recognize that we're
in a privileged position

00:49:59.310 --> 00:50:02.160
to do that, but that is our mentality.

00:50:02.160 --> 00:50:05.963
Yeah, I think the conversations
we've been having,

00:50:08.080 --> 00:50:09.980
the word opportunity comes up a lot.

00:50:09.980 --> 00:50:12.930
And when we talk to our team we're trying

00:50:12.930 --> 00:50:15.083
to convey that to them as well that,

00:50:16.760 --> 00:50:18.940
let's look at, let's
be as smart as we can,

00:50:18.940 --> 00:50:21.363
let's be as safe as we can,

00:50:23.020 --> 00:50:25.330
and then within that context
let's look at the things

00:50:25.330 --> 00:50:29.660
that we can actually do, the
things that we have control of,

00:50:29.660 --> 00:50:33.690
and let's look for as many
opportunities that this

00:50:33.690 --> 00:50:38.653
extremely challenging and
scary situation grants us.

00:50:40.840 --> 00:50:43.100
Because we can still make videos,

00:50:43.100 --> 00:50:45.597
it's an opportunity for us to do that.

00:50:45.597 --> 00:50:47.760
I think the main opportunities are not

00:50:47.760 --> 00:50:50.600
to capitalize on anything,
I mean opportunity

00:50:50.600 --> 00:50:52.550
in the sense of service.

00:50:52.550 --> 00:50:56.700
An opportunity to serve
our immediate families

00:50:56.700 --> 00:50:58.950
because we're spending
so much time together.

00:51:02.014 --> 00:51:05.640
Our friends and loved ones
via phone calls and chats.

00:51:07.380 --> 00:51:10.980
Serving our Mythical team,

00:51:10.980 --> 00:51:13.310
some of which we can give work to,

00:51:13.310 --> 00:51:16.800
others don't have as much to do from home

00:51:16.800 --> 00:51:19.583
because of the nature of what
they were doing at Mythical,

00:51:21.244 --> 00:51:23.870
there's just little
for them to do at home.

00:51:23.870 --> 00:51:26.090
But givin' them an assurance that, hey,

00:51:26.090 --> 00:51:28.530
we're gonna keep putting out content

00:51:28.530 --> 00:51:31.170
in the most creative way we can

00:51:31.170 --> 00:51:34.640
in order to continue to support

00:51:37.290 --> 00:51:41.260
all of our team and to
keep 'em on the payroll,

00:51:41.260 --> 00:51:43.633
just to be blunt about it.

00:51:45.190 --> 00:51:49.270
But also to create content as a way to,

00:51:49.270 --> 00:51:51.280
an opportunity to serve

00:51:52.690 --> 00:51:54.833
all the Mythical Beasts, to serve you.

00:51:56.580 --> 00:51:59.980
Because, I mean, we're
continuously blown away

00:51:59.980 --> 00:52:03.080
with the opportunity we have

00:52:03.080 --> 00:52:05.210
to make things that connect with you.

00:52:05.210 --> 00:52:07.690
We come back to it a lot on this podcast,

00:52:07.690 --> 00:52:10.250
that it's an amazing
privilege to be able to create

00:52:10.250 --> 00:52:12.790
something that's a source
of light in your life

00:52:12.790 --> 00:52:17.470
and when we all, everybody
on Earth is experiencing

00:52:17.470 --> 00:52:21.510
some level of shadow
being cast by this virus

00:52:21.510 --> 00:52:23.690
over their lives, some of them it feels

00:52:23.690 --> 00:52:27.740
like a complete eclipses, and
if they're a Mythical Beast

00:52:27.740 --> 00:52:32.740
and we can give 'em 15 minutes,

00:52:34.200 --> 00:52:38.140
25 minutes with Good Mythical
MORE every single weekday,

00:52:38.140 --> 00:52:40.350
if we can give 'em a vlog,

00:52:40.350 --> 00:52:42.620
if we can give 'em these conversations,

00:52:42.620 --> 00:52:45.210
it's a privilege to be able to do that.

00:52:45.210 --> 00:52:49.440
And it's exciting to
know that we can do it.

00:52:49.440 --> 00:52:53.220
It's very engaging for me to say,

00:52:53.220 --> 00:52:55.400
you know what, I can
make this not just for,

00:52:55.400 --> 00:52:59.530
it's not about the business,
it's not about the money

00:52:59.530 --> 00:53:02.790
except in supporting our team

00:53:04.450 --> 00:53:05.820
and our families--

00:53:05.820 --> 00:53:06.653
And we've talked about this before--

00:53:06.653 --> 00:53:09.600
But it's about supporting
the Mythical Beasts, too.

00:53:10.810 --> 00:53:12.660
You know, one of the things that we

00:53:14.550 --> 00:53:19.547
have just realized over the years is that

00:53:22.380 --> 00:53:27.380
we never set out to do
anything except create, right?

00:53:29.100 --> 00:53:30.590
Even years ago, we just were like, okay,

00:53:30.590 --> 00:53:34.180
we're just gonna make videos,
oh, there's an audience here,

00:53:34.180 --> 00:53:36.220
and then people started talking about

00:53:36.220 --> 00:53:39.100
the effect that the regular content

00:53:39.100 --> 00:53:41.720
coming into their homes had on people,

00:53:41.720 --> 00:53:44.030
helping people get through things.

00:53:44.030 --> 00:53:47.150
I think for many years I always
felt really self-conscious

00:53:47.150 --> 00:53:49.517
and kinda uneasy about
that, because I was like,

00:53:49.517 --> 00:53:53.397
"Well, that's not what we're trying to do.

00:53:53.397 --> 00:53:54.910
"We're just trying to make you laugh,"

00:53:54.910 --> 00:53:59.000
but eventually I kinda
just accepted the reality

00:53:59.000 --> 00:54:02.570
that making people laugh is therapeutic,

00:54:02.570 --> 00:54:05.420
it's good, it's like
you got to embrace that.

00:54:05.420 --> 00:54:07.600
This is helpful, and I think that

00:54:07.600 --> 00:54:11.360
in a moment like this
when you can't do anything

00:54:11.360 --> 00:54:14.780
except consume content, for some people

00:54:14.780 --> 00:54:15.910
you're in a situation where it's like,

00:54:15.910 --> 00:54:18.180
okay, I can clean, I can work out,

00:54:18.180 --> 00:54:20.600
I can take a hike, I can make myself food.

00:54:20.600 --> 00:54:25.410
But really a lot of us are
just consuming content,

00:54:25.410 --> 00:54:27.690
reading books, listening to podcasts,

00:54:27.690 --> 00:54:29.790
watching internet videos, watching movies.

00:54:30.700 --> 00:54:33.483
And those are,

00:54:35.130 --> 00:54:37.330
yeah, it's an opportunity,
and it's an opportunity

00:54:37.330 --> 00:54:39.111
that we take very seriously,

00:54:39.111 --> 00:54:42.690
so we're completely committed,
just like Link said,

00:54:42.690 --> 00:54:45.860
we're completely committed
to continuing to bring you

00:54:47.230 --> 00:54:50.363
the content that we
have always brought you.

00:54:51.360 --> 00:54:53.800
It's gonna look a little
bit different, of course,

00:54:53.800 --> 00:54:54.900
and we'll be talking about how

00:54:54.900 --> 00:54:56.840
that's gonna affect GMM
and some other things,

00:54:56.840 --> 00:55:00.770
and hopefully this is not
too long of a period of time

00:55:00.770 --> 00:55:04.320
and we'll get back to a
somewhat normal schedule soon.

00:55:04.320 --> 00:55:07.330
But our intention is that we recognize

00:55:07.330 --> 00:55:10.000
that this is something
that people kinda count on,

00:55:10.000 --> 00:55:12.733
especially during this time, and we wanna,

00:55:13.890 --> 00:55:17.140
not just for the sake of
our business and our company

00:55:17.140 --> 00:55:22.140
kinda remaining buoyant,
but also to serve you guys.

00:55:23.380 --> 00:55:26.860
Kinda the additional
thing I've been thinking

00:55:26.860 --> 00:55:29.850
is, again, and I'm trying
to evaluate whether or not

00:55:29.850 --> 00:55:31.460
what part of this comes
from a healthy place

00:55:31.460 --> 00:55:34.390
and what part comes from my sort of,

00:55:34.390 --> 00:55:37.720
my own personal struggles with being

00:55:37.720 --> 00:55:41.630
so performance-based,
kinda having that mindset

00:55:41.630 --> 00:55:44.220
of always wanting to do-do-do

00:55:44.220 --> 00:55:46.353
and finding my self-worth in what I do.

00:55:48.940 --> 00:55:53.290
I've seen this virus,
I mean a lot of people

00:55:53.290 --> 00:55:55.410
are taking advantage of this time

00:55:55.410 --> 00:55:58.380
to not only do things for people,

00:55:58.380 --> 00:56:01.540
but to do things that are super-cool

00:56:01.540 --> 00:56:03.430
and get them noticed, right?

00:56:03.430 --> 00:56:05.780
And sometimes I start thinking like,

00:56:05.780 --> 00:56:08.010
okay, all right, we've
got GMM, that's great,

00:56:08.010 --> 00:56:08.843
but we've always had that.

00:56:08.843 --> 00:56:10.360
We've got your Biscuits, that's great,

00:56:10.360 --> 00:56:11.670
but we've always had that.

00:56:11.670 --> 00:56:12.990
We've got the Mythical Kitchen stuff

00:56:12.990 --> 00:56:14.863
that we recently added to the mix,

00:56:16.060 --> 00:56:17.053
all this stuff.

00:56:18.200 --> 00:56:20.367
But I start feeling this
pressure to be like,

00:56:20.367 --> 00:56:23.453
"But what else could we do?" you know?

00:56:24.720 --> 00:56:25.830
And I think that some of that could be,

00:56:25.830 --> 00:56:27.980
and I also feel like from
a personal standpoint,

00:56:27.980 --> 00:56:30.870
which is like, oh, you've got
all this time on your hands.

00:56:30.870 --> 00:56:34.333
Well you know what, you should
really figure yourself out.

00:56:35.220 --> 00:56:37.533
This is an opportunity
to really accelerate

00:56:37.533 --> 00:56:39.440
that work you've been doing in therapy

00:56:39.440 --> 00:56:40.293
and you need to,

00:56:41.410 --> 00:56:42.940
this is when you really need to sit down

00:56:42.940 --> 00:56:44.960
and figure out what your
values are and your goals

00:56:44.960 --> 00:56:47.960
and all this stuff and the
things that you keep putting off,

00:56:47.960 --> 00:56:50.650
kinda like getting your mind
straight about this stuff.

00:56:50.650 --> 00:56:53.880
So I've felt sort of
this intense pressure,

00:56:53.880 --> 00:56:56.512
I've actually been overwhelmed
by it a couple of times,

00:56:56.512 --> 00:56:59.450
and I've felt myself waking
up in the middle of the night

00:56:59.450 --> 00:57:01.347
thinking about, "What am I gonna do?

00:57:01.347 --> 00:57:02.810
"What are we gonna do?"

00:57:02.810 --> 00:57:03.967
Uh-huh.

00:57:03.967 --> 00:57:05.380
"What are we gonna do to
take advantage of this?"

00:57:05.380 --> 00:57:10.330
Not in a slimy
get-more-attention kinda way,

00:57:10.330 --> 00:57:12.423
but it's just like, you know,

00:57:13.500 --> 00:57:16.150
being in this sort of
desperate, desperate times

00:57:16.150 --> 00:57:19.620
but being in a position where
we can bring people stuff,

00:57:19.620 --> 00:57:21.050
feeling this pressure to be like,

00:57:21.050 --> 00:57:25.190
what else could we do and
what else could I do to

00:57:26.600 --> 00:57:30.510
deal with myself and all
the problems that I have?

00:57:30.510 --> 00:57:32.553
Yeah, I mean, for me,

00:57:34.090 --> 00:57:36.190
recording this podcast is a relief for me

00:57:36.190 --> 00:57:39.550
because I now know that we're wrapping up

00:57:39.550 --> 00:57:42.783
and we actually got one in the
can and were able to do it.

00:57:43.786 --> 00:57:48.340
We're gonna record the
first Good Mythical Morning

00:57:48.340 --> 00:57:53.280
split-screen ever in a few
hours, I'll feel much better.

00:57:53.280 --> 00:57:56.380
But yesterday I was completely overwhelmed

00:57:56.380 --> 00:57:58.690
setting up all the technical
aspects and I just,

00:57:58.690 --> 00:58:01.290
I felt very buried so I
didn't have the margin

00:58:01.290 --> 00:58:03.880
to think that much about those things

00:58:03.880 --> 00:58:05.820
but I definitely understand.

00:58:05.820 --> 00:58:07.083
I think the,

00:58:10.180 --> 00:58:13.290
I wanted to read a quote form an article

00:58:13.290 --> 00:58:14.960
that I think speaks directly to this,

00:58:14.960 --> 00:58:17.870
and also hopefully can
serve as encouragement

00:58:17.870 --> 00:58:20.963
to you listening, too.

00:58:22.240 --> 00:58:24.780
I was reading a Washington Post article

00:58:24.780 --> 00:58:28.880
which came across Twitter,
and in that article which,

00:58:28.880 --> 00:58:30.000
I'm sorry, I can't
remember what it's called,

00:58:30.000 --> 00:58:35.000
but there's a rabbi,
Rabbi Yosef, was quoted

00:58:35.720 --> 00:58:38.620
from his Facebook page
where he had written

00:58:38.620 --> 00:58:41.820
to his L.A. congregation, but then

00:58:41.820 --> 00:58:44.380
it was put in the Washington Post article

00:58:44.380 --> 00:58:47.503
and had an impact on me
in terms of my mindset.

00:58:49.420 --> 00:58:53.110
He said: Every hand that we don't shake

00:58:53.110 --> 00:58:56.010
must become a phone call that we place.

00:58:56.010 --> 00:58:58.400
Every embrace that we avoid

00:58:58.400 --> 00:59:02.950
must become a verbal expression
of warmth and concern.

00:59:02.950 --> 00:59:06.570
Every inch and every foot
that we physically place

00:59:06.570 --> 00:59:10.230
between ourselves and
another must become a thought

00:59:10.230 --> 00:59:14.493
as to how we might help that
other should the need arise.

00:59:16.050 --> 00:59:18.570
So yeah, I think that

00:59:20.380 --> 00:59:22.083
this morning, you know,

00:59:22.920 --> 00:59:25.080
in an effort to keep my routine going

00:59:25.080 --> 00:59:28.120
and to start my day with some sanity,

00:59:28.120 --> 00:59:31.530
I've been trying to do my
workouts in the garage.

00:59:31.530 --> 00:59:34.020
And this morning I was halfway through it

00:59:34.020 --> 00:59:38.163
and this Golden Retriever
just runs up into my garage.

00:59:39.400 --> 00:59:41.090
And then it ran back,

00:59:41.090 --> 00:59:43.050
I petted the Golden
Retriever, it ran back out

00:59:43.050 --> 00:59:45.580
and then this woman who's
a neighbor that I see

00:59:45.580 --> 00:59:49.480
but never talk to is walking
by, and I take the earbud out

00:59:49.480 --> 00:59:53.009
and we had a conversation, and it was,

00:59:53.009 --> 00:59:54.570
"How are you doing?"
From 10 feet away.

00:59:54.570 --> 00:59:56.737
Yeah, from 10 feet away, and I was like,

00:59:56.737 --> 00:59:59.380
"Oh, maybe I shouldn't
have touched her dog."

00:59:59.380 --> 01:00:02.130
I'd only pet it once but
I'm not gonna sit here

01:00:02.130 --> 01:00:04.690
and love up on the dog.

01:00:04.690 --> 01:00:05.883
But we had a,

01:00:08.860 --> 01:00:10.860
it was a friendly
conversation, it was short,

01:00:10.860 --> 01:00:15.110
but there was an undertone of
care that I gave and received

01:00:15.110 --> 01:00:18.060
that was extremely powerful, that I never,

01:00:18.060 --> 01:00:20.889
and it was simple, it was,

01:00:20.889 --> 01:00:22.867
"This is so surreal, isn't it?

01:00:22.867 --> 01:00:25.427
"Do you have everything you need?

01:00:25.427 --> 01:00:27.030
"It's actually a beautiful morning,"

01:00:27.030 --> 01:00:28.210
and then that was it, you know?

01:00:28.210 --> 01:00:30.390
But there was a connection that otherwise

01:00:30.390 --> 01:00:32.820
would never have been made.

01:00:32.820 --> 01:00:37.250
And I drew strength from giving that care

01:00:37.250 --> 01:00:38.703
and also receiving it.

01:00:41.380 --> 01:00:43.053
It doesn't have to be the big things,

01:00:43.053 --> 01:00:46.053
it can be those small
interactions, you know?

01:00:47.158 --> 01:00:49.300
And I think about all the
opportunities that I have

01:00:49.300 --> 01:00:51.610
within my own home with my family.

01:00:51.610 --> 01:00:53.250
The amount of time that
we're spending together

01:00:53.250 --> 01:00:55.733
is utterly unprecedented,

01:00:56.610 --> 01:00:58.520
and there's a lotta pitfalls
associated with that.

01:00:58.520 --> 01:01:00.770
I'm sure we'll get into all that later.

01:01:00.770 --> 01:01:02.890
We need to wrap up for
the sake of uploading

01:01:02.890 --> 01:01:05.080
this huge freakin' file.

01:01:05.080 --> 01:01:08.223
But I just wanted to
leave it at that quote.

01:01:09.150 --> 01:01:10.453
Yeah, I think, you know,

01:01:12.380 --> 01:01:15.820
we're gonna see, I think we are seeing

01:01:15.820 --> 01:01:18.350
the best and the worst
in people right now.

01:01:18.350 --> 01:01:20.900
There are people who are
thinking about themselves.

01:01:23.000 --> 01:01:25.870
People hoarding, people buying up things,

01:01:25.870 --> 01:01:27.410
people price-gouging.

01:01:27.410 --> 01:01:30.850
But I think that the majority of people

01:01:30.850 --> 01:01:32.800
are thinking about their fellow person.

01:01:38.700 --> 01:01:39.980
I have hope that in the end

01:01:39.980 --> 01:01:42.650
that this is gonna bring us together,

01:01:42.650 --> 01:01:46.720
even though we are, especially
in this country, divided.

01:01:46.720 --> 01:01:50.963
Again, like I said, even about
our perspective on a virus.

01:01:52.422 --> 01:01:54.493
A virus has no political affiliation.

01:01:56.040 --> 01:01:58.860
The virus doesn't care about
whether you're in a red state

01:01:58.860 --> 01:02:01.690
or a blue state, but
still, for some reason

01:02:01.690 --> 01:02:04.710
we just, we're so
polarized in this country

01:02:04.710 --> 01:02:06.720
that we filter everything through that.

01:02:06.720 --> 01:02:07.940
We can't hear a piece of news

01:02:07.940 --> 01:02:10.920
without filtering it through the source.

01:02:10.920 --> 01:02:13.753
But I think that this is,

01:02:14.960 --> 01:02:17.120
it's an unprecedented situation,

01:02:17.120 --> 01:02:19.530
but it's an unprecedented opportunity

01:02:19.530 --> 01:02:23.650
to think about what you can do

01:02:23.650 --> 01:02:25.160
for your fellow human.

01:02:25.160 --> 01:02:28.530
I would say to love
yourself and to love others.

01:02:28.530 --> 01:02:29.363
Yeah.

01:02:30.376 --> 01:02:34.371
Okay, I think this went okay.

01:02:34.371 --> 01:02:37.160
I think it went well.
You got a rec, though?

01:02:37.160 --> 01:02:37.993
Yeah, I do.

01:02:39.580 --> 01:02:40.720
I've been telling you about this,

01:02:40.720 --> 01:02:43.393
you haven't responded
to me about this yet.

01:02:44.275 --> 01:02:45.108
Oh, I know what you're gonna say.

01:02:45.108 --> 01:02:46.290
Now first of all, I will say

01:02:46.290 --> 01:02:50.760
you do need to watch "Lord
of the Rings," do that.

01:02:50.760 --> 01:02:52.220
That's clear.

01:02:52.220 --> 01:02:54.700
You gotta watch "Tiger King" on Netflix.

01:02:54.700 --> 01:02:56.450
Everyone's been talkin' about this.

01:02:58.300 --> 01:02:59.660
Many of you have probably
already watched it,

01:02:59.660 --> 01:03:01.810
you've heard about
people talkin' about it.

01:03:04.770 --> 01:03:06.610
And it's not for everybody,
let me just say that.

01:03:06.610 --> 01:03:09.080
It's not for everybody.
It's a documentary.

01:03:09.080 --> 01:03:10.580
It's a docu-series--
I didn't know that.

01:03:10.580 --> 01:03:12.740
About a,

01:03:12.740 --> 01:03:15.240
not just, one guy, the "Tiger King",

01:03:15.240 --> 01:03:18.457
is just a dude who
raises tigers, you know?

01:03:18.457 --> 01:03:21.920
And like a sketchy situation,
like the whole home zoo

01:03:21.920 --> 01:03:23.830
and exotic animals and that kind of thing.

01:03:23.830 --> 01:03:28.520
But it's the world of that guy

01:03:28.520 --> 01:03:30.370
and then other people like him,

01:03:30.370 --> 01:03:35.370
but it's also kind of a murder
mystery, and it's all real.

01:03:37.130 --> 01:03:39.000
I watched the first episode last night

01:03:39.000 --> 01:03:43.723
because you told me to watch
it and I was laughing out loud.

01:03:45.100 --> 01:03:48.733
The first episode, real
fresh to me, amazing.

01:03:50.820 --> 01:03:53.570
There were some hilarious edits.

01:03:53.570 --> 01:03:55.420
Getting to know him as a character,

01:03:55.420 --> 01:03:59.910
and then it takes some turns
and it's like, it's serious.

01:03:59.910 --> 01:04:01.900
But I've never laughed so hard--

01:04:01.900 --> 01:04:03.500
You wait for episode two.

01:04:03.500 --> 01:04:05.603
Like everything that you thought,

01:04:07.099 --> 01:04:09.687
like they go a level deeper
and you're like, "No.

01:04:09.687 --> 01:04:11.060
"No, no, no, no, no, that can't be true."

01:04:11.060 --> 01:04:12.610
Yeah, they keep peelin,
it's like an onion.

01:04:12.610 --> 01:04:13.781
They keep peeling back
layers, I'm like, "What?"

01:04:13.781 --> 01:04:17.523
The onion goes down like four
more layers on episode two.

01:04:19.160 --> 01:04:20.347
You gotta watch it,

01:04:20.347 --> 01:04:22.150
"Tiger King".
You gotta watch it.

01:04:22.150 --> 01:04:25.940
On Netflix, for me, the
thing that I was thinking

01:04:25.940 --> 01:04:27.167
when I was watching it is I was like,

01:04:27.167 --> 01:04:28.747
"Man, we need to find a story like this,"

01:04:28.747 --> 01:04:31.707
it was just inspirational
for me to think about--

01:04:32.930 --> 01:04:35.360
It was like over decades.
Yeah, I know.

01:04:35.360 --> 01:04:37.210
I don't know if he was
just utilizing the footage

01:04:37.210 --> 01:04:38.990
because the guy had a
cameraman follow him forever

01:04:38.990 --> 01:04:41.510
and then somebody came
in and like, anyway.

01:04:41.510 --> 01:04:42.470
They get into it a little better.

01:04:42.470 --> 01:04:43.530
Yeah, I agree.

01:04:43.530 --> 01:04:45.130
If you can't tell, I agree with the rec.

01:04:45.130 --> 01:04:47.360
Okay, so we're gonna be doing this

01:04:47.360 --> 01:04:49.703
for the foreseeable future from our homes.

01:04:51.870 --> 01:04:54.081
Let us know what you thought
about this conversation

01:04:54.081 --> 01:04:57.550
and how you're dealing with
all this, hashtag #EarBiscuits.

01:04:57.550 --> 01:04:58.910
We'll be talking about it.
Hashtag #EarBiscuits.

01:04:58.910 --> 01:05:01.220
We'll be speaking at you next week.

01:05:01.220 --> 01:05:02.053
Love ya.

